# Lab 7 — Create a Custom Reusable Skill — the Ghostwriter

**Topic 03:** Building Reusable Skills and Schedules  |  **Day 2**  |  **Approx. 45 min**  |  **Course:** AI Agents with Gemini Spark (C1259)

> **Connected project:** this lab adds one more automation to the **Brightwave AI operations assistant** you began in Lab 1. If you fell behind, use the supplied Brightwave sample data to rejoin at this lab.

## Goal

Turn a proven instruction into a named, reusable skill and run it repeatedly with different inputs.

## What you'll build

A saved, named 'Brightwave Ghostwriter' skill, tested on two different inputs.

**Tools and techniques:** Reusable skills, saving instructions, naming and describing, style capture, testing

## Prerequisites

- Labs 1-6 complete, especially your refined instructions from Lab 3 and Lab 5.
- A couple of content topics in mind to test the skill on.

## Steps

### Step 1

Start from a strong instruction. Write one that drafts a short piece of content in your studio's voice.

Instruction to give the agent (paste into Gemini Spark):

```text
Draft a 120-word LinkedIn post about <TOPIC> in Brightwave's voice: friendly, concrete, no hype, one clear call to action.
```

### Step 2

Run it once and refine until the style is right — this becomes the body of your skill.

### Step 3

Save it as a reusable skill. Give it a clear name and a one-line description of what it does and when to use it.

Instruction to give the agent (paste into Gemini Spark):

```text
Name: Brightwave Ghostwriter | Description: Drafts a short social post in Brightwave's voice from a topic.
```

### Step 4

Make the input a placeholder so the skill is reusable: the topic should be something you supply each time you run it.

### Step 5

Test the skill on a first topic. Check the draft matches the voice and the word count.

### Step 6

Run the SAME skill on a second, different topic. Confirm it stays in voice without you re-typing the instruction.

### Step 7

Add one guardrail to the skill's instruction: never invent statistics or client names; leave a placeholder instead.

### Step 8

Note two other repeatable tasks in your week that would make good skills — you will need candidates for chaining in Lab 9.

## Test it

You can run your saved Ghostwriter skill by name on any topic and get an in-voice draft of the right length, without re-typing the instruction, and it never invents facts.

## Troubleshooting

- **The skill drifts off-voice on some topics.** Put the voice rules inside the skill's saved instruction, not just in a one-off prompt, and add an example.
- **You can't make the topic a placeholder.** Save the instruction with a clear placeholder like <TOPIC> and supply it when you run the skill.
- **The skill invented a statistic or client name.** Add the guardrail 'never invent statistics or client names; use a placeholder and flag it' to the skill's instruction.

## Challenge

Build a second skill — a 'Meeting-notes summariser' — and give it the same voice, so your skills feel consistent.

## Reflection

LO5 — Which repeatable task in your week is the best candidate to become your next reusable skill?

## Deliverable

Save your output — it becomes part of your **AI operations assistant**, the connected toolkit you consolidate, monitor and plan to scale in Lab 12.

